{"id":"ccb09c49-0468-4bb3-aff1-67d48f5547cd","object":"chat.completion","created":1785018247,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    required_fields = ['passing', 'failing', 'required_difference', 'unresolved_difference']\\n    for f in required_fields:\\n        if f not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {f}\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(case[f], list):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Field {f} must be a list\\\")\\n\\n    passing_set = set(case['passing'])\\n    # Compute ordered difference (items in failing but not in passing, preserving order in failing)\\n    D = []\\n    for item in case['failing']:\\n        if item not in passing_set:\\n            D.append(item)\\n\\n    required_set = set(case['required_difference'])\\n\\n    def test(subset):\\n        # Determine if subset causes failure (i.e., contains all required differences)\\n        if required_set.issubset(set(subset)):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        else:\\n            return 'PASS'\\n\\n    # If no changes, return empty result with 0 tests\\n    if not D:\\n        return {'isolated_difference': [], 'tests': 0}\\n\\n    # Verify that the full set causes failure (should by construction)\\n    if test(D) != 'FAIL':\\n        # Not expected; but if it happens, cannot isolate\\n        raise ValueError('Full difference does not cause failure')\\n\\n    # ddmin algorithm\\n    changes = D\\n    n = 2\\n    tests = 0\\n\\n    while len(changes) > 1:\\n        # Partition into n subsets (approximately equal size)\\n        k = len(changes)\\n        indices = [i * k // n for i in range(n + 1)]\\n        subsets = [changes[indices[i]:indices[i+1]] for i in range(n)]\\n\\n        # Test each subset\\n        reduced = None\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            tests += 1\\n            if test(subset) == 'FAIL':\\n                reduced = subset\\n                break\\n\\n        if reduced is not None:\\n            changes = reduced\\n            n = 2\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Test complements of each subset\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            # Complement relative to changes\\n            complement = [x for x in changes if x not in set(subset)]\\n            tests += 1\\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\\n                reduced = complement\\n                break\\n\\n        if reduced is not None:\\n            changes = reduced\\n            n = 2\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Increase granularity\\n        n = min(n * 2, len(changes))\\n\\n    # Final verification that isolated set still causes failure (should be minimal by algorithm)\\n    if test(changes) != 'FAIL':\\n        raise ValueError('Isolated set does not cause failure')\\n\\n    return {\\n        'isolated_difference': changes,\\n        'tests': tests\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-DD-02. The description: \"Isolate a minimal causal difference between passing and failing configurations under the paper's delta-debugging invariant.\" So we are to perform delta debugging (ddmin) on the difference between failing and passing sets. The case payload has: passing (list of strings), failing (list of strings), required_difference (list), unresolved_difference (list). The task is to compute the isolated difference (minimal subset of the difference that causes failure) and number of tests (presumably number of tests performed during ddmin). The output schema requires \"isolated_difference\" (array of strings) and \"tests\" (integer).\n\nWe need to implement ddmin algorithm on the set of items that are in failing but not in passing. Let's call that set D = set(failing) - set(passing). Actually the payload shows failing includes all passing plus extra x items. So D = [item for item in failing if item not in passing]. But we need to maintain order? The description says \"Retain source order through every subset and complement.\" So we should treat D as a list in the order they appear in failing? Or the order in failing beyond passing? Typically delta debugging uses a list of changes. The required_difference and unresolved_difference seem to be given? But we are to compute ourselves. The fixtures show required_difference and unresolved_difference as empty? Actually they are present. Possibly they are the expected output? But we are not to use them for computation; we need to compute our own.\n\nThe algorithm: ddmin takes a set of changes (the difference) and a test function that returns PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED. We don't have a real test oracle; we must simulate or infer from payload? The payload only has lists, not test results. Wait, the problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" The case payload has passing, failing, required_difference, unresolved_difference. The required_difference and unresolved_difference might be hints or expected results? But we need to compute based on some oracle? Without a test oracle, we cannot run ddmin. But maybe the required_difference and unresolved_difference are part of the input that defines the oracle? The description: \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing. Run ddmin only on this difference. Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" So we need to know for any subset of the difference whether it causes failure. How? Possibly the required_difference is the minimal set that must be present to cause failure, and unresolved_difference is additional differences that don't affect outcome? Actually looking at the fixtures: payloads have required_difference and unresolved_difference. For case 0: required_difference [\"x4\",\"x5\"], unresolved_difference []. For case 1: [\"x2\",\"x4\"]. For case 2: [\"x2\",\"x5\"]. So these are given. They likely represent the actual minimal causal difference. But we are not supposed to return them directly; we must compute via ddmin algorithm using an oracle that we can derive from these? Perhaps the oracle is defined as: a subset S of D causes failure if and only if S contains all elements of required_difference. And unresolved_difference are changes that are not needed. But we should not hardcode; we should compute based on given data. Alternatively, maybe the required_difference and unresolved_difference are from a previous step and we are to use them as part of the case? The problem statement says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we must check that all fields exist. Then we compute.\n\nSince we have no external test oracle, the only way is to use the given required_difference and unresolved_difference to simulate test outcomes. The ddmin algorithm would run tests on subsets. We can implement a test function that returns PASS if the subset does not contain all elements of required_difference, FAIL if it contains all, and UNRESOLVED if something else? But unresolved_difference indicates changes that don't affect outcome? In delta debugging, we often have a monotonic property: if a set causes failure, any superset also causes failure. The required_difference is the minimal set. The unresolved_difference might be changes that are irrelevant. So we can implement oracle: given a subset S, test = FAIL if required_difference is subset of S, else PASS. And unresolved_difference doesn't matter. However, the problem says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" Possibly there is an UNRESOLVED state for cases where the test outcome is ambiguous. But in typical ddmin, there are only PASS and FAIL. UNRESOLVED might be for cases where we cannot determine. But given the input, we can assume it's always PASS or FAIL based on whether required_difference is contained.\n\nAlternatively, we could compute the isolated difference by simply returning required_difference? That would be too trivial. The problem expects us to implement ddmin algorithm to find that minimal set. So we need to actually run ddmin on the difference D, with a test function that uses required_difference as the condition for failure. The number of tests would be the count of test function invocations during ddmin.\n\nNote: The output schema \"isolated_difference\" must be an array of strings. \"tests\" integer.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) in Python, using standard library only. Must return dictionary with keys \"isolated_difference\" and \"tests\". Must not mutate case. Raise ValueError for invalid cases (e.g., missing fields).\n\nWe'll write ddmin algorithm: given a list of changes (in order), we try to reduce to minimal subset that still causes failure. The algorithm splits into smaller subsets and tests. We'll implement the classic ddmin from \"Simplifying and Isolating Failure-Inducing Input\" by Zeller.\n\nPseudocode:\ndef ddmin(changes, test):\n    # changes: list\n    # test: function that returns 'PASS', 'FAIL', or 'UNRESOLVED'\n    n = 2\n    while len(changes) > 1:\n        subsets = [changes[i*len(changes)//n:(i+1)*len(changes)//n] for i in range(n)]\n        complement = [changes[i*len(changes)//n: (i+1)*len(changes)//n] for i in range(n)]? actually we need to test each subset and its complement.\n        # Standard ddmin tests each subset; if any subset fails, reduce to that subset. Else if complement fails, reduce to complement. Else increase n.\n        # We need to keep order.\n    return changes\n\nBut we also need to count tests. We'll add a counter.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use list slicing, ensure order.\n- Test function: given subset S, check if required_difference is subset of S. If yes, return 'FAIL', else 'PASS'. We'll ignore UNRESOLVED.\n- But what about unresolved_difference? Possibly we should treat it as irrelevant; if a subset contains unresolved_difference but not required_difference, it's PASS. So ok.\n- Also need to consider that the complement test may also be needed. In standard ddmin, we test each subset and its complement. Actually typical ddmin tests each subset; if none fails, it tests the complement of each subset (i.e., 1-complement). If a complement fails, reduce to that complement. If none, increase granularity.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\ndef ddmin(ls, test, counter):\n    # ls: list of items\n    n = 2\n    while len(ls) > 1:\n        # split into n subsets\n        subset_size = len(ls) // n\n        subsets = [ls[i*subset_size:(i+1)*subset_size] for i in range(n)]\n        # if len(ls) not divisible, last subset gets remaining? Actually we want equal-sized as possible. Standard: partition into n subsets, with possibly different sizes. Use floor division and distribute remainder.\n        # We'll implement proper partition: start indices.\n        # We'll use: sizes = [len(ls)//n + (1 if i < len(ls)%n else 0) for i in range(n)] and then partition.\n        # But for simplicity, we can do: indices = [i*len(ls)//n for i in range(n+1)]; subsets = [ls[indices[i]:indices[i+1]] for i in range(n)]\n        # That's fine.\n        # Test each subset\n        any_fail = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            result = test(subset)\n            counter[0] += 1\n            if result == 'FAIL':\n                ls = subset\n                any_fail = True\n                break\n        if any_fail:\n            n = max(2, n-1)  # reset granularity? Actually after reduction, we go back to 2.\n            continue\n        # test complements\n        for subset in subsets:\n            complement = [x for x in ls if x not in subset]  # but careful: duplicates? items are distinct.\n            result = test(complement)\n            counter[0] += 1\n            if result == 'FAIL':\n                ls = complement\n                any_fail = True\n                break\n        if any_fail:\n            n = max(2, n-1)\n            continue\n        # increase granularity\n        n = min(n*2, len(ls))\n    return ls\n\nWe need to ensure not to test empty list? If ls becomes empty, return [].\n\nBut we also need to guarantee that the initial set D causes failure. The test on full D should return FAIL. We can check at start: if test(D) is not FAIL, then no failure-inducing difference? But given inputs, it should be.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Validate payload has keys: passing, failing, required_difference, unresolved_difference. All should be lists.\n- Compute D = [item for item in failing if item in failing and item not in passing] but preserve order of failing? Actually we need to retain source order: the failing list is presumably ordered. So D should be a list of items in failing that are not in passing, in the order they appear in failing. Because the difference is the set of changes that are added to passing to get failing. So we can iterate through failing and keep items not in passing (set of passing for fast check).\n- But ensure uniqueness? Items may be duplicates? Probably not.\n- Then we need to define test function that uses required_difference. But required_difference is given; we can check if set(S) >= set(required_difference). \n- However, note that the required_difference may include items that are not in D? Possibly not; from fixtures, required_difference is subset of D.\n- The test function should also handle UNRESOLVED? Not needed.\n- Run ddmin with count.\n- Return {\"isolated_difference\": list(ordered minimal set), \"tests\": count}.\n- Important: The output must match schema: additionalProperties false, so we only include those two keys.\n\nEdge cases: D may be empty? Then no difference; but problem likely has non-empty. If D empty, maybe return empty list and tests=0.\n\nRaise ValueError if any required field missing.\n\nImplementation code within solve(case) function.\n\nNow produce the JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as string.\n\nWe must escape the source string properly for JSON embedding. Use json.dumps to encode.\n\nWrite final response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1278,"completion_tokens":3121,"total_tokens":4399,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2441},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":126},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}